<problem>


<!-- <title>Extrema warmup with graph</title> -->

<variantControl numVariants="1" />

<setup>

<function name="f" variables="x">e ^ (-x^4/24-x^3/18+x^2/2)</function>
<derivative name="fprime" variables="x">$f</derivative>

<math name="fprime_poly" expand="true">-x^3 / 6 − x^2 / 6 + x</math>
<math name="fprime_poly_factored">−x/6 (x - 2)(x + 3)</math>

<math name="fprime_poly_partial_factored">−x/6 (x^2 + x − 6)</math>
(
  
<math name="cp1">-3</math>
<math name="cp2">0</math>
<math name="cp3">2</math>
<math name="critical_points">$cp1, $cp2, $cp3</math>

<subsetOfReals name="interval1">(-infinity, $cp1 )</subsetOfReals>
<subsetOfReals name="interval2">($cp1 ,$cp2 )</subsetOfReals>
<subsetOfReals name="interval3">($cp2 ,$cp3 )</subsetOfReals>
<subsetOfReals name="interval4">($cp3, infinity)</subsetOfReals>
<math name="f_cp1">$$f($cp1 )</math>
<math name="f_cp2">$$f($cp2 )</math>
<math name="f_cp3">$$f($cp3 )</math>
<math name="f_n1">$$f(-1)</math>
<math name="f_3">$$f(3)</math>
<math name="three">3</math>
<math name="f_n4">$$f(-4)</math>
<math name="f_1">$$f(1)</math>

</setup>



<p>We will explore the maxmima and minima of the function
<me>f(x)= $f</me>
For this warm-up problem, we'll plot the function so you can see the answers even before doing the math.
</p>


<graph size="large" aspectRatio="12/5" xmin="-5" xmax="4" ymin="-2" ymax="16" showNavigation="false">
  <yLabel><m>f</m></yLabel>
<function extend="$f" />

</graph>




<exercise>
<p>Calculate the derivative of <m>f</m>.</p>

<p>

<m>f'(x)=</m>
<answer>
  <mathInput name="mi_fprime" />
  <award><when>$mi_fprime = $fprime and <boolean symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true">$mi_fprime != 0</boolean></when></award>
</answer>
</p>


<p>The first step in locating maxima and minima is finding critical points of <m>f</m>.  Since the derivative exists everywhere, the only critical points are those where <m>f'(x)=0</m>.</p>

<p>You should have calculated that <m>f'(x)</m> is a polynomial times an exponential function (actually, a polynomial times <m>f</m> itself).  Can the exponential function ever be zero? 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput>
<choice credit="1">no</choice>
<choice>yes</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
.  Therefore, the only critical points are where the polynomial is zero, i.e., where

<answer>
<award>$fprime_poly </award>
</answer>
 <m>= 0</m>.</p>
  
<p>To find these critical points, factor out an <m>x</m> from the polynomial (even better, factor out <m>-\frac{x}{6}</m>), so that the remaining factor is a quadratic polynomial.  The factored equation becomes</p>


<p>


<answer name="partialFactor">
  <mathInput name="miPartialFactor" />
  <award>
    <when>
      <hasSameFactoring>
        $miPartialFactor
        $fprime_poly_partial_factored
      </hasSameFactoring>
    </when>
  </award>
  <award>
    <when>
      <hasSameFactoring>
        $miPartialFactor
        $fprime_poly_factored
      </hasSameFactoring>
    </when>
  </award>
  <award name="award1" credit="0.2">$fprime_poly</award>
</answer>
   <m>= 0</m>.</p>

<feedback condition="$award1">This does equal the quadratic polynomial, but it is not factored.</feedback>



<p>Now, factor the quadratic polynomial (or use the quadratic formula) to find all the critical points. Critical points: <m>x=</m> 
<answer>
<award unorderedCompare="true" matchPartial="true">$critical_points </award>
</answer>

</p>

<p>(Separate multiple answers by commas.)</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
Find the intervals where <m>f</m> is increasing and decreasing.  In this case, the critical points divide the number line into four intervals.  In each interval, you can test <m>f'(x)</m> for a point in the interval to determine if <m>f'(x)</m> is positive or negative in the interval.  From left to right, list the intervals and whether <m>f</m> is increasing or decreasing in the interval.

<p>For the interval 
<answer>
  <mathInput name="mi1" />
  <award referencesAreResponses="$mi1">
    <when><subsetOfReals>$mi1</subsetOfReals> = $interval1</when>
  </award>
</answer>
, <m>f</m> is 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput>
<choice credit="1">increasing</choice>
<choice>decreasing</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
.
</p>
<p>

For the interval 
<answer>
  <mathInput name="mi2" />
  <award referencesAreResponses="$mi2">
    <when><subsetOfReals>$mi2</subsetOfReals> = $interval2</when>
  </award>
</answer>
, <m>f</m> is 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput>
<choice credit="1">decreasing</choice>
<choice>increasing</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
.
</p>
<p>

For the interval 
<answer>
  <mathInput name="mi3" />
  <award referencesAreResponses="$mi3">
    <when><subsetOfReals>$mi3</subsetOfReals> = $interval3</when>
  </award>
</answer>
, <m>f</m> is 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput>
<choice credit="1">increasing</choice>
<choice>decreasing</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
.
</p>
<p>

For the interval 
<answer>
  <mathInput name="mi4" />
  <award referencesAreResponses="$mi4">
    <when><subsetOfReals>$mi4</subsetOfReals> = $interval4</when>
  </award>
</answer>
, <m>f</m> is 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput>
<choice credit="1">decreasing</choice>
<choice>increasing</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
.</p>


<hint>
You can write the intervals as <m>(a,b)</m> or as <m>a &lt; x &lt; b</m>.  Or if an interval extends to infinity, you can write it, for example, as <m>(-\infty, b)</m> or <m>x &lt; b</m>.
</hint>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>Local maxima can only occur where <m>f</m> changes from increasing to decreasing (which can happen only at critical points).  Find the local maxima of <m>f</m>.</p>

<p><m>f</m> has local maxima at: <m>x=</m> 
<answer>
<award>$cp1 </award>
</answer>
 and at <m>x=</m> 
<answer>
<award>$cp3 </award>
</answer>

</p>

<p>(Enter in increasing order.)</p>

<p>What values does <m>f(x)</m> attain at the local maxima?</p>

<p>

<m>f\bigl(</m>
<mathInput name="micp1" />
<m>\bigr)=</m> 
